    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Filing a homeowners claim sounds simple until you are in the middle of it. Policies are long, adjusters change, and the first estimate can leave out much of what it will cost to put your home back together. Dallas Public Adjuster provides policyholder representation on <strong>residential property insurance claims in Dallas</strong> and across Dallas County, from Highland Park and University Park to Richardson, Garland, Mesquite, Irving, and Grand Prairie. We help homeowners understand their coverage, document the full scope of the damage, and present the claim clearly to the insurance company.</p>

<h2>Residential Claims We Handle in Dallas</h2>
<h3>Roof and hail damage</h3>
<p>North Texas sits in the southern Plains hail belt. NOAA records list 841 hail reports in Dallas County since 1955, with the season peaking in April and May. Hail can bruise shingles, crack ridge caps, dent gutters and vents, and damage siding, screens, and fences. See <a href="/services/hail-damage-claims/">hail damage claims</a> and <a href="/services/roof-damage-insurance-claims/">roof damage insurance claims</a>.</p>
<h3>Storm and wind damage</h3>
<p>Severe straight-line winds can lift shingles, peel back flashing, and topple trees onto homes. On May 28, 2024, the National Weather Service found winds up to 95 mph in Garland and up to 90 mph in Richardson. Tornadoes, such as the EF3 that crossed north Dallas on October 20, 2019, can cause structural damage. See <a href="/services/wind-damage-claims/">wind damage claims</a>.</p>
<h3>Water leaks and burst pipes</h3>
<p>Plumbing failures, appliance leaks, slab leaks, and freeze damage are common homeowners claims. Coverage often depends on whether the water was sudden and accidental. See <a href="/services/water-damage-claims/">water damage claims</a>.</p>
<h3>Fire and smoke</h3>
<p>Fire losses involve structural repair, smoke and soot cleaning, odor removal, water damage from firefighting, contents, and living expenses while the home is being repaired. See <a href="/services/fire-damage-claims/">fire damage claims</a>.</p>
<h3>Mold-related damage</h3>
<p>Mold that follows a covered water loss may be covered in part, but many policies cap or exclude mold. Acting quickly after a leak helps limit both the damage and the dispute.</p>

<h2>What Makes Dallas Homes Different</h2>
<p>Housing across Dallas varies widely, and the details matter when an adjuster writes an estimate. Many homes are brick veneer with composition shingle roofs. Tudor and Craftsman homes in the Park Cities often have steep roofs, complex flashing, and specialty materials. Older pier-and-beam houses in Lakewood, the M Streets, Lower Greenville, and Oak Cliff have crawlspaces and original plumbing that can be hard to inspect. Mid-century ranches in Lake Highlands and Richardson may have long, low roofs with multiple layers or older decking.</p>
<p>The region’s expansive Blackland Prairie clay soils also cause foundation movement and cracking. Foundation movement from soil is usually excluded, but damage caused by a covered event, such as water from a plumbing leak, may be treated differently. Check your policy.</p>

<figure><img src="/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/Property-Inspection.webp" alt="Inspector standing on the roof of a brick home with a ladder against the eave" width="640" height="427" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>A thorough roof inspection on a brick-veneer home looks at shingles, flashing, vents, gutters, and elevations.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>Understanding Your Homeowners Policy</h2>
<p>Before negotiating any claim, it helps to know what your policy actually says. Key sections include:</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Policy item</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Coverage A (Dwelling)</td><td>Pays to repair the house itself; often used to calculate percentage wind/hail deductibles</td></tr>
<tr><td>Coverage B (Other Structures)</td><td>Fences, detached garages, sheds, and pergolas</td></tr>
<tr><td>Coverage C (Personal Property)</td><td>Contents such as furniture, clothing, and electronics</td></tr>
<tr><td>Coverage D (Loss of Use)</td><td>Additional living expenses if the home cannot be lived in during repairs</td></tr>
<tr><td>Replacement cost vs. ACV</td><td>Determines whether depreciation is held back and released after repairs</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ordinance or law</td><td>May pay for code-required upgrades, up to a limit</td></tr>
<tr><td>Exclusions and endorsements</td><td>Flood, mold, wear and tear, cosmetic roof damage, and other limits</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>For more on depreciation, read <a href="/replacement-cost-vs-actual-cash-value-texas/">replacement cost vs. actual cash value in Texas</a>.</p>

